ccurring is a woman has hpv  31 she clears  it and then she gets re-infected with an  hpv  such as 52. that's one  possible explanation for this data  the other possible explanation is that  we have to remember  that many women have multiple  genotypes especially those who don't  have  cin3 or cancer and it is possible  that a woman at year one could have hpv  16 and 52  but 52 was expressed at a very low level  beneath  the cutoff of the pcr assay  and the following year hpv 16  is either cleared or expressed at a low  level that's not detected  but hpv 52 is being expressed a little  more robustly and therefore can be  picked up with the assay  so it could be one or the other  wonderful thank you so much for that  answer now dr wright  how does extended genotyping fit in with  the american cancer society's new  screening guidelines  uh the new screening guidelines  recommend primary hpv screening  beginning at age 25 and extending to age  65 with a number of caveats about the  age at which you cut off depending on  how many times you've been negative  but essentially it's primary screening  they do not go into the role  of extended genotyping  so with extended genotyping  as we looked at the algorithms it's  clear that you can use it for primary  screening and that appears to have  benefit but it's currently not in the  american cancer society's guidelines  thank you for that answer dr wright so  we do have time for a few more questions  and we want to thank our audience for  all these great questions coming in  um i do want to remind you that any  questions that we are unable to answer  today and those that come in during the  on-demand period  will be addressed by dr wright via the  email address that you provided  at the time of registration so please do  not hesitate to keep these questions  coming in  so dr wright let's move on to our next  question  the clearance of hpv could be due to a  low hpv count uh would this be a false  negative  i'm sorry could you repeat that again oh  absolutely so the clearance of hpv  could be due to a low hpv count  would this be a false negative  it would be right it would be a low hpv  viral load is one reason that you  may end up being hpv negative we see  this  not infrequently for those of you who  are cytopathologists and you get the hpv  results on your patients as you're  reviewing abnormal  paps you clearly see  women who are hpv positive  they'll go a year or two and be hpv  negative and then they become hpv  positive  and it's not infrequent and it's  believed that  many of these women simply are shedding  hpv  at relatively low  numbers of a low viral load uh  the good news is is that those women  with low viral loads  tend to be at low risk for developing  high-grade disease  is that a false negative of the assay  well i guess you could interpret it that  way  um it's also  she's hpv positive but with a low  load  now our next question how long does a  positive  hpv infected sample in a liquid-based  cytology preservative  be maintained to be positive  how long can it be maintained a lot of  that depends  on temperature and the way it is stored  certainly we are finding with archival  specimens  that with time and time being years  that they begin to get lower  viral loads but certainly for a number  of months  they are relatively  cape or over many months  you can get hpv out of them that is not  necessarily the way in which the package  inserts  reads they all have timelines but in  research studies  people go back to samples which are  three four years old  and tend to get the same results when  they test them with different tests  now doctor right if a woman is hpv  positive for multiple strains when  tested  will all of the strains be reported or  just the most prevalent  that is going to be determined by what  you request  your laboratory to do certainly with the  bd assay  you could get reported in the groupings  which i've shown you throughout this  talk  and that  would require you to request the lab  to report out those strains  they're not going to at least i it's new  it's hard to know what all the labs are  going to do  but i don't think a lot of labs are  going to report out routinely  all the different strains in an hpv  report  they'll probably report out hpv-positive  hpv-16 hpv-18  and if you request it tell you whether  or not a woman's got  31. and the other one which is quite  important  grouping is 56 59 66  because they're such low risk  now dr wright what is if you could  explain what is the full  meaning of hpv  i'm sorry what is the full  meaning of hpv uh  as far as what the letters stand for  it's human papilloma virus  wonderful thank you so much okay oh go  ahead  no i just i'm not sure that was what the  question meant  um if you are still in the if  um whoever entered in that question if  you are still in the audience please  feel free if there is another meaning  that you meant for that question please  feel free to go ahead and put it in the  in the q a box that way we could  that way we could be able to answer your  question a little bit more specifically  for you  um so dr wright let's move on to uh  to our next question will hpv  testing with extended genotyping be  available for patients to collect at  home  currently we do not have an approved  hpv test which can be collected at home  there are a number of groups working on  this  but there are no approved tests in the  united states  once tests begin to become approved  the question will be will the  manufacturers  feel that they should run a clinical  trial  to get that as an fda approved test  and for bd and the unclarity answer  on clarity assay i really don't have any  insight as to whether or not that will  happen  and what bd thinks about that  great thank you so much dr wright um so  it looks like we didn't get any  more clarification on that last question  um  so we'll go ahead and thank you so much  everyone for the questions that you have  brought in  um thank you again dr wright do you have  any final comments for our audience  i think so the  field of cervical cancer screening is  moving quite rapidly certainly  we now have primary hpv screening  listed as the primary way you should be  screening patients  the real issue is the number of  colposcopies  which is are generated both when you do  primary hpv screening  as well as when you do co-testing you  really  generate large numbers of colposcopies  not just at the baseline visit  but also on follow-up visits and the  real  goal over the next several years  is to develop methodologies such as  extended genotyping  which are going to allow us to identify  which women are at greatest risk  and get those women into colposcopy and  yet take  other women at low or risk and  let them go on to some form of follow-up  preferably extended follow-up at three  years  rather than at one year so i think  there's going to be a lot of  new data coming out a lot of new  methodologies  unfortunately things are going to become  more and more  confusing for most of us and that's why  the risk-based approach is so important  and why the asccp has moved on to that  approach  i'd like to thank everybody for tuning  in today and listening to this  presentation  and thank you very much  thank you dr wright and while you were  doing your final comments we actually  had one more question come in that we  would love to be able to ask you  um does the vaccine  create any mutation of clinically  significant strains of hpv  no there have been no reports  of new strains of hpv  secondary to the vaccine there has been  some data suggesting that we get  different vax different hpv  genotypes by prevalence in vaccinated  women but that's exactly what you would  expect  because 16 and 18 are reduced  dramatically in vaccinated women  as well as using the nanovalent vaccine  other genotypes are being reduced so  when you look at a cross section of a  population  you would expect to pick up these other  genotypes  more commonly but that does not mean  that  new genotypes are being developed  or that there's even replacement  of genotypes by a non-vaccine genotype  has not  been shown to be true in enough  studies that people accept it  thank you dr wright for squeezing that  last question in there for us  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions that we did not have time for  today and those submitted during the  on-demand period will be addressed by dr  wright via the contact information that  you provided at the time of registration  we would like to once again thank dr  wright for his time today his important  research  and we would also like to thank labberts  and our sponsor bd  for underwriting today's educational  webcast  you can view the webinar on demand  labyrinths will alert you via email when  it's available for replay  that's all for now thank you for joining  us and until next time  bye-bye
 

